We tested whether a second application of native layer 23 could predict ordinary text without a router, task prompt or constrained outputs. The first pass supplied a full-vocabulary distribution. A second pass supplied a normalized query and keys to a 256-token continuous cache of earlier states and their observed successor tokens [16]. Three fitted scalars controlled temperature and interpolation. All predictions at position $t$ used only keys from positions strictly earlier than $t$. Training used 96 separate WikiText-2 training articles. The reported incremental checks ran the predictor on six actual prefixes without their future tokens.

Across 40 test articles (10,240 targets) and 120 existing retention passages (9,328 targets), the candidate scored a pooled NLL of 3.00688 versus 3.00592 for Base. It made one more correct top-1 prediction overall (8,320 versus 8,319). It gained 17 top-1 predictions on the original suite and lost 16 on WikiText. It improved likelihood on exactly 20 of 40 articles. All six incremental predictions matched the teacher-forced readout. The candidate used 11.66 s and 1.18 GiB peak versus 12.95 s and 1.82 GiB for Base. The result did not meet the required likelihood and top-1 gains on both corpora. The base-initialized student used 8-bit frozen weights outside the final layer. Both quantization and memory contribute to the system comparison.

A second fit used the distributions of pinned Qwen3.5-2B at 32,768 training positions from 1,024 documents. The student repeated its final shared native layer and trained 1,204,224 adapter parameters in 800 updates. Targets retained the teacher's 32 most probable tokens and the total probability of the tail. Constructing these targets took 134.53 s and fitting took 129.72 s. A 16-position projection tile allowed the full-vocabulary calculation to fit within 8 GiB. Training sampled 76,800 positions including repeats.

Across 52 additional complete evaluation excerpts from different WikiText articles, the distilled model lowered the document loss on 50 and raised it on two (two-sided sign test $p=6.12 times 10^(-13)$). It scored 3.06172 versus 3.16533 nats/token, with 5,646 versus 5,555 correct next tokens. Pooling this panel with the existing 120 passages gave 3.03556 versus 3.09002 nats/token, 9,515 versus 9,451 correct, 13.03 versus 14.73 s and 1.19 versus 1.82 GiB peak. All six incremental checks agreed. On the existing suite alone, NLL rose from 2.98256 to 2.99821 and correct predictions fell from 3,896 to 3,869. General prose and scientific exposition lost 0.04252 and 0.03491 nats/token respectively. Code descriptions lost 27 top-1 predictions and technical explanations lost 25. The preregistered retention gate kept the distilled weights out of the release.

A further native model uses the ordinary final-layer hidden state to learn a per-token interpolation between the first pass and a second adapted pass of the same layer. We enrolled 2,048 training excerpts and 128 additional evaluation passages from separate books, documentation files and science articles. During fitting, the two teachers' likelihoods of the observed next token determine their relative supervision. During inference the gate has access only to preceding text. Selective distillation in translation provides a precedent for varying how much an individual training target contributes [27]. The published method and the learned inference gate here are distinct.

#figure(image("raw-language.svg", width: 88%),caption: [Token-weighted difference in negative log likelihood between each candidate and its own paired Base control. Values below zero favor the candidate. Bars are percentile intervals from 2,000 within-domain resamples of complete documents.])

*Selective refinement from two teachers.* The earlier distilled model gained on WikiText and lost 27 top-one decisions on the 120-passage retention suite. The next student starts from pinned Base. It keeps the first 23 layers at eight bits, freezes the ordinary first application of the final BF16 attention layer and applies the same pretrained layer again with rank-48 adapters. A scalar predicted from the normalized first-pass hidden state interpolates between the ordinary state and the second-pass state before vocabulary projection. Both passes run at every position. A smaller interpolation coefficient reduces the change of state; it does not skip computation.

Two teachers supplied top-32 probabilities, remaining mass and gold-token log probability at the same 65,536 positions across 2,048 training documents. At each position a sigmoid of the 2B teacher’s gold-token log-probability advantage over ordinary 0.8B determined the teacher mixture. The loss also included 18% ordinary next-token cross-entropy. The model never sees a gold-token score at inference. The 541,697 trainable parameters received 1,200 updates and 115,200 sampled positions including repeats. Teacher construction took 266.34 and 135.69 s. Frozen student features took 80.82 s and optimization 94.31 s [27].

#figure(image("selective-sources.svg", width: 97%), caption:[Change from untouched Base on the fresh sources. Each point combines all enrolled passages of the named source. Negative loss differences and positive changes in the number correct favor the recurrent model. The two books each contribute multiple excerpts.])

On 36,126 targets across 300 passages the model achieved NLL 3.06194 versus 3.08052, with 15,076 versus 15,006 correct top-one predictions. Warm prediction took 20.81 versus 24.21 s and peak allocation was 1.18 versus 1.84 GiB. All three enrolled suites separately showed lower loss, more correct next tokens, shorter time and a lower peak. The fresh 128 passages gained 18 correct predictions (5,573 vs 5,555) and 79 passages had lower loss versus 49 with higher loss (paired sign test $p=0.0101$). The 52 WikiText articles gained 37 correct and the 120 retention passages gained 15. All six live incremental predictions matched their full forward passes; the six observed interpolation coefficients ranged from 0.0135 to 0.0868.

The fresh loss gain was 0.00599 nats per token against the registered 0.015, and the new WikiText gain was 0.03761 against 0.04. Natural history lost four correct predictions and science lost one. Fiction and natural history each had about 0.0033 nats/token higher NLL; general prose and scientific exposition also had small loss increases. The per-domain gate closed replacement of the ordinary language route and the matched-depth successor. Item-level uncertainty is conditional on these documents, which include multiple passages from the same source. The command-line generator can load the sealed experimental weights for ordinary raw continuation.
